Narrow combine.c's PLUS->IOR conversion in GCC 2.7.2's simplify_rtx: keep it,
except for (plus REG CONST_INT).

ADOPTED 2026-09-25 under owner ruling bcdc1648e (docs/ORACLE-COMPILER.md
"Owner ruling 2026-09-25 (second batch)"; docs/grind/decisions.md 2026-09-25
OWNER RULING -- oracle compiler adoption (second batch)). It replaces
tools/cc1-no-plus-to-ior.patch, which deleted the conversion outright.

This is THE patch that defines the Bushido Blade 2 oracle compiler. It is
applied by tools/build_oracle_cc1.sh to a pristine checkout of
decompals/mips-gcc-2.7.2 @ 43d1cdb67ed135879869b5266f01efaaada5e35a inside a
scratch tree, together with the host-ABI crash-fix declaration
tools/cc1-reorg-negate-rtx-decl.patch. tools/gcc-2.7.2/ is gitignored, which
is why both patches live here, tracked, instead of as edits in the compiler
tree.

WHAT IT DOES
  Stock GCC rewrites `a + b` into `a | b` whenever it can prove the two
  operands have no bits in common (`nonzero_bits(a) & nonzero_bits(b) == 0`).
  This patch keeps that rewrite, except when the addition is a plain
  register plus a constant, `(plus REG CONST_INT)`. That is the machine's
  add-immediate and address form, and an IOR of an opaque register cannot
  simplify further. Every other disjoint-bits addition is rewritten, as stock
  does: shift and mask expressions, register plus register.
  `simplify_rtx` measures 0x3a6a (stock 0x3a11; the retired no-rewrite
  patch 0x39ab).

WHY (docs/ORACLE-COMPILER-STUDY-2026-09-25.md)
  The shipped game shows both behaviours:
  - site A (func_800174F4, `(plus (reg) (const_int 4))`): the original KEPT
    addiu;
  - site C (`main`, `(plus (ashift ...) (const_int 128))`) and func_80073C78
    (`(plus (subreg du0) (reg u))`): the original performed the rewrite.
  Neither stock ("always") nor the retired patch ("never") explains all
  three. This condition does, and it builds the whole current tree to the
  oracle SHA1.

  It is the best fit to the evidence, NOT a recovered historical compiler.
  The study's `exprop` condition also fits every compiled site and stays a
  live alternative. A later discriminating site reopens the choice for the
  owner (ruling item (D)). The `|`-for-`+` spelling stays refused in both
  directions.

  This does not loosen the no-compiler-divergence rule. This patch and the
  owner-approved crash-fix declaration are its only in-scope amendments.

diff --git a/combine.c b/combine.c
--- a/combine.c
+++ b/combine.c
@@ -3618,10 +3618,15 @@ simplify_rtx (x, op0_mode, last, in_dest)
       /* If we are adding two things that have no bits in common, convert
 	 the addition into an IOR.  This will often be further simplified,
 	 for example in cases like ((a & 1) + (a & 2)), which can
-	 become a & 3.  */
+	 become a & 3.
+
+	 A register plus a constant is left alone: it is the add-immediate
+	 and address form, and an IOR of an opaque register cannot simplify.  */

       if (GET_MODE_BITSIZE (mode) <= HOST_BITS_PER_WIDE_INT
+	  && ! (GET_CODE (XEXP (x, 0)) == REG
+		&& GET_CODE (XEXP (x, 1)) == CONST_INT)
 	  && (nonzero_bits (XEXP (x, 0), mode)
 	      & nonzero_bits (XEXP (x, 1), mode)) == 0)
 	return gen_binary (IOR, mode, XEXP (x, 0), XEXP (x, 1));
       break;
